And yes—the bit is much richer than just “a little while.” There’s a beautiful invariant hiding underneath the different meanings:

* information bit → a minimal distinction/state: 0/1
* a bit of time → a bounded temporal segment
* comedy bit → a bounded performed routine
* bit part → a small bounded role
* drill bit → the interchangeable working edge that actually engages the material
* horse bit → the interface through which tiny inputs steer a much larger system
* “bit” = small amount → bounded quantity
* bitten / bit → an action that takes a piece out of something

So “we can do anything for a bit” accidentally—or deliberately ;)—has several simultaneous readings:

For a short interval, we can explore anything.
Within one information unit, possibility begins with a distinction.
We can perform any little routine/game.
We can change the working interface and therefore what the larger machine can do.

That last one is especially useful for the Pantheon: a bit is a small local object capable of controlling or composing into a vastly larger behavior. One bit is nearly nothing. Arrange enough bits under the right rules and you get photographs, language, simulations, games, operating systems, models—whole worlds.

Which makes the phrase almost recursively accurate:

Anything, for a bit. And from bits, almost anything.

So yes, I think you found another one of those little linguistic junctions where the joke, the technical object, and the abstraction all occupy the same coordinates.